```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Water‑Themed Joke Generator</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
            background: #e0f7fa;               /* light aqua */
            color: #004d40;
            text-align: center;
            padding-top: 5rem;
        }
        button {
            background: #0097a7;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 0.8rem 1.5rem;
            font-size: 1rem;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
            transition: background 0.2s;
        }
        button:hover {
            background: #00796b;
        }
        #joke {
            margin-top: 2rem;
            font-size: 1.2rem;
            max-width: 600px;
            margin-left: auto;
            margin-right: auto;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>💧 Water‑Themed Joke Generator 💧</h1>

<button id="tellJokeBtn">Tell me a joke</button>

<p id="joke"></p>

<script>
    // 1️⃣ Our list of four water‑themed jokes
    const jokes = [
        "Why do fish never do well at school? Because they're always swimming below sea level!",
        "What do you call a nervous waterfall? A little bit of a splash‑tastic panic!",
        "Why did the ocean break up with the lake? It needed some space… a whole ocean’s worth!",
        "How does a raindrop ask for a date? It says, “Can I drizzle with you tonight?”"
    ];

    // 2️⃣ Function that picks a random joke and displays it
    function showRandomJoke() {
        const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
        const jokeEl = document.getElementById('joke');
        jokeEl.textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
    }

    // 3️⃣ Wire the button click to the function
    document.getElementById('tellJokeBtn')
            .addEventListener('click', showRandomJoke);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
1. **HTML** – A button (`#tellJokeBtn`) and an empty paragraph (`#joke`) where the selected joke will appear.  
2. **CSS** – Light‑blue background, a teal button with a hover effect, and simple typography.  
3. **JavaScript** –  
   * Stores four water‑themed jokes in the `jokes` array.  
   * `showRandomJoke()` picks a random index (`Math.random() * jokes.length`) and puts that joke into the paragraph.  
   * The button’s `click` event is bound to `showRandomJoke()`.

Save the code as `index.html` (or any name you like) and open it in a web browser – each click will give you a new, random water‑themed joke. Enjoy!
